GitHub: https://github.com/OpenVDN/vdn-minimax-h3
Project: https://openvdn.github.io/
Paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20744
ComfyUI port: https://github.com/Saganaki22/ComfyUI-VDN-H3

LATEST MEANINGFUL UPDATE
The Video DeltaNet paper was posted to arXiv on 2026-09-17. The
official project previously shipped code/weights and added SGLang
Diffusion support on 2026-09-14. The practical new reason to care is
that the research is now formally published and a consumer-ComfyUI
path is mature enough to test, including an INT8 ConvRot stage and
recent v1.5.x fixes.

WHAT IT DOES
VDN replaces expensive long-range softmax attention in MiniMax H3 with
a hybrid architecture: exact/local softmax for nearby detail plus
bidirectional linear Video Delta Attention memory for long-range
temporal context. The released checkpoint supports T2VA, I2VA, L2VA
and FL2VA with audio.

WHY TECHNICALLY INTERESTING
This attacks the actual scaling bottleneck for long generated video
rather than merely distilling the same dense architecture. Long-range
attention cost becomes linear in clip length while local interactions
remain exact. Combined with 8-step DMD distillation and SGLang,
OpenVDN reports 14.4 s of 768p video in ~9 s end-to-end on 8=C3=97B200
after warm-up; denoising is 6.9 s. Crucially for us, the same
architecture can be tested on consumer GPUs through the community
ComfyUI port.

INPUTS -> OUTPUTS
text / first frame / last frame / first+last frame -> 768p video with audio=
.

AVAILABILITY
Official training code, inference code and weights are public. SGLang
Diffusion supports the model. A native community ComfyUI
implementation reproduces the hybrid-attention math without modifying
ComfyUI core.

LICENSE
Official VDN training/inference code: Apache-2.0. Community ComfyUI
port: Apache-2.0. Model weights inherit the MiniMax H3 Community
License rather than Apache; commercial/territorial restrictions must
be checked before production use.

HARDWARE / COST
Headline realtime result: 8=C3=97B200. Official single B200 MXFP8
denoising: ~47.6 s for the 8-step model; 2=C3=97B200 ~25.9 s; 4=C3=97B200 ~=
13.1
s; 8=C3=97B200 ~6.9 s. Community ComfyUI port reports RTX 5090 1280=C3=9773=
6 /
145 frames / 8 steps at ~2:15 sampling. Its INT8 ConvRot stage cuts
branch storage ~4.3 -> 2.2 GB and reportedly lowers peak VRAM during
loading by ~4.7 GB. The author says a 736p INT8 stack can fit 12=E2=80=9316=
 GB
cards, though this is not the fastest MiniMax-H3 route on constrained
consumer GPUs.

MATURITY
RUNNABLE / research-production infrastructure.

RELEVANCE
Very high for generated-media factories, especially persistent hosts
where we want longer coherent scenes, controllable first/last frames
and cheap iteration. It is less directly useful for live
conversational avatars unless we have datacenter-class GPUs, but
highly relevant to the premium stochastic-render layer.

EXACT EXPERIMENT
Take one canonical AGI Everyone character reference and one 14 s
Performance-IR scene. Render identical prompt/reference/seed
conditions through (A) our current H3 path and (B) VDN-H3 8-step via
ComfyUI. Measure identity embedding drift, first/last-frame adherence,
audio coherence, temporal artifacts, wall time, VRAM and cost. Then
repeat at 30=E2=80=9360 s via continuation to see whether the linear-memory
architecture actually improves long-horizon identity/scene stability
rather than only speed.

REPLACES / COMPLEMENTS
Potential replacement for dense MiniMax H3 in the premium video
renderer. Complements Performance IR, Character Asset Graph and
HyperFrames/OpenShorts rather than replacing them.

VERDICT: TEST NOW.

